hls: a kept-alive connection that a failed request closed is not kept

A segment read to its end on a kept-alive connection leaves the playlist
with input_read_done set, and the next segment is requested on that
connection. When the request fails, open_url_keepalive() closes the
connection; on AVERROR_EXIT (the caller's interrupt callback, as when a
player closes during the request) read_playlist_data() returns at once and
input_read_done stays set with no input. The next call of the read
callback, which comes right away (avio_feof() refills the buffer once its
EOF flag is set: hls_read_packet() and the nested demuxers call it after a
failed read), hit av_assert0(v->input) and aborted the process. Reloads
that fail on an interrupt while the next segment is waited for leave the
same state after a segment was skipped.

Treat a playlist without input as having no kept connection: the next
segment is opened anew, as after a seek.

diff --git a/libavformat/hls.c b/libavformat/hls.c
--- a/libavformat/hls.c
+++ b/libavformat/hls.c
@@ -1987,6 +1987,13 @@ static int read_playlist_data(void *opaque, uint8_t *buf, int buf_size)
     int segment_retries = 0;
     struct segment *seg;
 
+    /* A request that failed after the last segment was read to its end on
+     * the kept-alive connection closed that connection (an interrupted
+     * keep-alive request does, then returns AVERROR_EXIT): nothing is kept
+     * any more, and the next segment is opened anew. */
+    if (!v->input)
+        v->input_read_done = 0;
+
     if (c->http_persistent && v->input_read_done) {
         av_assert0(v->input);
         ret = reload_playlist(v, c);
